-## Subtopics
-
-### Single 2D Projection Voxelization
-Uses only one view. Cheaper but prone to cracks. [Single 2D Projection Voxelization](3482-single-2d-projection-voxelization.md)
-
-### Orthographic Multi-View Voxelization
-Uses more than 3 views for higher accuracy. More expensive. [Orthographic Multi-View Voxelization](3892-orthographic-multi-view-voxelization.md)
-
-### Volumetric Representation via Depth Map Registration
-Uses depth maps from arbitrary views. Different aggregation strategy. [Volumetric Representation via Depth Map Registration](6435-volumetric-representation-via-depth-map-registration.md)
+**Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
+- **Performance:** Requires 3 render calls per object. Cost grows with mesh complexity [S1, unofficial].
+- **Dynamic Objects:** If separating dynamic and static objects, dynamic voxel grids must be cleared every frame, requiring separate grids [S1, unofficial].
+- **Precision:** Ensure the voxel-space matrix correctly handles the coordinate mapping to avoid off-by-one errors at boundaries [Parent].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** $O(N \cdot R^2)$ per pass, where $N$ is triangle count. Total $3 \times$ cost of single pass [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(R_x R_y R_z)$ for the grid [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Performance:** 3 render calls per object. Expensive for complex models [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Eliminates cracks from single-view projection [S1, S2, unofficial]. Simple to implement [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** High computational cost. Requires separate grids for dynamic/static objects if optimized [S1, unofficial].
-*   **When to Use:** When visual fidelity (no cracks) is critical and performance allows.
-*   **When Not to Use:** Real-time applications with very high triangle counts and limited GPU bandwidth.
+- **Time Complexity:** $O(N \cdot P)$, where $N$ is the number of triangles and $P=3$ is the number of passes. Each pass is a standard rasterization operation.
+- **Memory Complexity:** $O(R_x \cdot R_y \cdot R_z)$ for the voxel grid. No additional large buffers if rendering directly to the grid.
+- **Performance:** Slower than single-pass methods due to 3x draw calls. Suitable for static or infrequently updated geometry. Can be optimized by LOD interleaving or resolution scaling [S1, unofficial].
+- **Strengths:** Eliminates cracks and gaps inherent in single-view projection; robust against orientation.
+- **Limitations:** High computational cost for complex dynamic scenes; requires separate handling for dynamic/static splits.
 
 ## Background
-Originated in real-time ray-traced voxel global illumination research [S1, unofficial]. Developed as a simple fix for cracks in voxel grids generated by single-axis projection [S1, unofficial]. Implemented in open-source projects like 3D-Mesh-Voxelizer [S2, unofficial].
+3-Pass Voxelization was introduced as a solution to the "cracks and missing data" problem in single-direction voxelization [S1, unofficial]. It is a foundational technique in real-time ray-traced voxel global illumination pipelines, where accurate occupancy is critical for light transport [S1, unofficial]. The method is widely used in GPU-based voxelizers where multiple render targets or atomic operations are available.
 
